.TH UM80 1 "November 2025" "um80 0.3.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
um80 \- Microsoft MACRO-80 compatible assembler for 8080/Z80
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B um80
[\fB\-v\fR]
[\fB\-o\fR \fIoutput.rel\fR]
[\fB\-l\fR \fIlisting.prn\fR]
[\fB\-g\fR]
[\fB\-t\fR]
[\fB\-\-aseg\fR]
[\fB\-\-dri\fR]
[\fB\-D\fR \fISYMBOL\fR[=\fIVALUE\fR]]...
[\fB\-I\fR \fIPATH\fR]...
.I input.mac
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B um80
is a Microsoft MACRO-80 compatible assembler that translates 8080/Z80 assembly
source files (.MAC) into relocatable object files (.REL). The output is compatible
with the
.BR ul80 (1)
linker.
.PP
The assembler supports both Intel 8080 and Zilog Z80 instruction sets, macros,
conditional assembly, and the full set of MACRO-80 directives.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v\fR, \fB\-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.TP
.BI \-o " output.rel"
Specify the output file name. Default is the input file name with a .rel extension.
.TP
.BI \-l " listing.prn"
Generate an assembly listing file showing source, generated code, and symbol table.
A field the linker has yet to finish shows the bytes the .REL carries (the
offset of a relocatable word; 0 for a field the linker computes or fills from
an external) and is marked after its last byte as MACRO-80 marks it:
\fB'\fR program relative, \fB"\fR data relative, \fB!\fR COMMON,
\fB*\fR external.
.TP
.B \-g\fR, \fB\-\-globals
Export all defined symbols as PUBLIC. This is useful for generating complete symbol
files for debugging. The linker can then output all symbols with their final
relocated addresses using the \fB\-S\fR option.
.TP
.B \-t\fR, \fB\-\-truncate
Cut every PUBLIC, EXTRN and module name in the .REL file to 6 characters, as
MACRO-80 does. Use it for an object that is to be linked with objects that
M80 assembled, when a PUBLIC or EXTRN name is longer than 6 characters.
.TP
.B \-\-aseg
Start the file in ASEG, so that an ORG is an absolute address, as in
Digital Research's MAC, which has no relocatable segments.
.TP
.B \-\-dri
Read the source as Digital Research's MAC and RMAC read it where they differ
from MACRO-80: a \fB$\fR inside a name is ignored, so NMB$LST and NMBLST are
one symbol, and \fBPUBLIC A$BC\fR writes ABC.  A \fB$\fR that starts a word
(the location counter), one in a quoted string or a comment, and one in the
arguments of a macro call or the list of an IRP or IRPC, which MAC reads as
text, are kept.
A macro body is matched to its parameters with MAC's names, which end at a
\fB$\fR, \fB_\fR or \fB.\fR, and with RMAC's rules for an \fB&\fR in a
string; only an argument that starts with \fB%\fR is a value, a
\fB<\fR or \fB>\fR in it is an operator, and a \fB%\fR with nothing
after it is an error (MACRO-80 passes 0); an item of
an IRP list ends at a comma, a blank or a tab is skipped at the start of
an item, and one anywhere else, or a \fB;\fR, in the list is an error, as
MAC and RMAC read it otherwise or flag it
(MACRO-80 ends an item at a comma, a \fB;\fR, a blank or a tab); the
arguments of a macro call end at a blank after one, and text after it
other than a comma is an error (MACRO-80 reads the blank as a comma); a
\fB"\fR in a macro call's arguments or an IRP list is text, not a quote; an IF that
a macro expansion, or one repetition of a REPT, IRP or IRPC, leaves open ends
with it; a label on the ENDM that ends a body is defined where the body ends;
and an IRPC with an empty string goes round once.
Without \fB\-\-dri\fR a \fB$\fR is part of the name, and a macro body is
read, as in MACRO-80.
The first word of a statement that is not an instruction, directive or
macro is a label, colon or not, in any column (\fBOBP DS 1\fR); a line
number in front of a statement, a line that starts with \fB*\fR and MAC's
assembly controls (\fB$\-MACRO\fR) are ignored.  Without \fB\-\-dri\fR a
label needs its colon, as in MACRO-80.
A \fB!\fR ends a comment and starts the next statement
(\fBNOP ;c! DB 1\fR is 00 01), and an ENDM after a \fB!\fR ends the
body being defined.  After a macro call the next statement is the one after
the first \fB!\fR after its arguments that follows a character other than a
blank, as in MAC: \fBMM ;c! DB 1\fR assembles the DB, and \fBMM A;c! DB
1\fR, \fBMM A ! DB 1\fR and \fBMM ;c ! DB 1\fR leave it out, with a
warning.  Without \fB\-\-dri\fR a comment
runs to the end of the line, as in MACRO-80.
An 8AH (a line feed with bit 7 set) inside a line is a line feed, the byte
0AH in a string, and an 8DH a carriage return that ends the statement, after
which the next item is taken for its line feed, as MAC reads them; MACRO-80
leaves out an 8AH and ends the line at an 8DH.
\fBPUSH A\fR and \fBPOP A\fR are PUSH PSW and POP PSW, and an expression may
have two register names in it; MACRO-80 flags both, and so does um80 without
\fB\-\-dri\fR.
MAC's relational operators \fB=\fR, \fB<\fR, \fB<=\fR, \fB>\fR, \fB>=\fR and
\fB<>\fR are EQ, LT, LE, GT, GE and NE, and HIGH and LOW apply to all that
follows them (\fBHIGH(X)+1\fR is HIGH(X+1)), as in MAC; MACRO-80 has no such
operator, and applies HIGH and LOW to the term after them.
An END in a MACLIB library ends the library, as in MAC and RMAC; MACRO-80
reads a MACLIB file as an INCLUDE file, whose END ends the source.
\fB\-\-dri\fR does not imply \fB\-\-aseg\fR (MAC's absolute sources) or
\fB\-t\fR (RMAC's six-character names).
A two-character string used as a value has its first character in the low
byte, as in MAC: \fB'AB'\fR is 4241H, and \fBDW 'AB'\fR is 41 42 (MACRO-80:
4142H, 42 41).
It does not yet follow MAC in two things: IF (MAC takes it as true only
when bit 0 is set), and a MACLIB library (MAC assembles none of its code or
data); see the EXTENSIONS document.
.TP
.BI \-D " SYMBOL" [= VALUE ]
Define a symbol before assembly begins. If no value is given, the symbol is
defined with value 1. Can be specified multiple times.
.TP
.BI \-I " PATH"
Add a directory to the include file search path. Can be specified multiple times.
.SH SOURCE FORMAT
Assembly source files consist of lines containing:
.IP \(bu 2
Labels (optional) followed by a colon.  The first word of a statement is
its operation, in column 1 or not (\fBNOP\fR in column 1 is an instruction),
except the name in front of an EQU, SET, DEFL, ASET or MACRO.  As in
MACRO-80, a statement whose first word is no instruction, directive or macro
is assembled as DB, with a warning (\fBLAB: 5,6\fR), so a label without a
colon is an error (see \fB\-\-dri\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
Instructions or directives
.IP \(bu 2
Comments starting with semicolon (;)
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
        ORG     0100H
START:  MVI     A,0         ; Initialize A
        LXI     H,BUFFER    ; Point to buffer
        CALL    INIT
        JMP     START
BUFFER: DS      100
        END     START
.fi
.RE
.SH DIRECTIVES
.SS Assembly Control
.TP
.B ORG \fIaddress\fR
Set the location counter to \fIaddress\fR.
.TP
.B END \fR[\fIaddress\fR]
End of source. Optional \fIaddress\fR specifies the entry point.
.SS Data Definition
.TP
.B DB \fIvalue\fR[,\fIvalue\fR]...
Define bytes. Values can be numbers or quoted strings.
.TP
.B DW \fIvalue\fR[,\fIvalue\fR]...
Define 16-bit words (low byte first).
.TP
.B DS \fIcount\fR
Define storage (reserve \fIcount\fR bytes).
.SS Symbol Definition
.TP
.B EQU \fIvalue\fR
Define a constant symbol (cannot be redefined).
.TP
.B SET \fIvalue\fR
Define a variable symbol (can be redefined with SET or DEFL).
.TP
.B DEFL \fIvalue\fR
Same as SET.
.SS Segments
.TP
.B CSEG
Select code segment (program-relative).
.TP
.B DSEG
Select data segment (data-relative).
.TP
.B ASEG
Select absolute segment.
.PP
As in MACRO-80, a segment directive goes on at the most of the locations set
in the segment (by ORG, or the end of a DS) and of those it was left at: after
an ORG back below an earlier ORG, not where the segment was left.
.TP
.B COMMON \fR/\fIname\fR/
Select or define a COMMON block. Each COMMON statement starts at the beginning
of its block, as in MACRO-80 (FORTRAN's rule).
.SS External Linkage
.TP
.B PUBLIC \fIsymbol\fR[,\fIsymbol\fR]...
Make symbols available to other modules.
.TP
.B EXTRN \fIsymbol\fR[,\fIsymbol\fR]...
Declare symbols defined in other modules.
.SS Macros
.TP
\fIname\fB MACRO \fR[\fIparam\fR[,\fIparam\fR]...]
Begin macro definition.
.TP
.B ENDM
End macro definition.
.TP
.B LOCAL \fIsymbol\fR[,\fIsymbol\fR]...
Declare local symbols within a macro.
.TP
.B EXITM
Exit macro expansion early.
.TP
.B <\fItext\fB>
In a macro argument or an IRP item: \fItext\fR as it is, its commas and
semicolons too.  The brackets go wherever they are in the argument
(\fBMM 1<2>3\fR passes 123, \fBMM <1,2>3\fR 1,23), and only the outer ones
(\fB<<A>>\fR is <A>), as in MACRO-80, MAC and RMAC.
.TP
.B %\fIexpression\fR
As a macro argument: the value of the expression when the macro is called,
as digits in the current radix, written as MACRO-80 writes them (with
.B .RADIX 16,
26 is 1A and 160 is 0A0).
With
.B \-\-dri
only an argument that starts with % is a value, as in MAC.
.SS Repeat Blocks
.TP
.B REPT \fIcount\fR
Repeat block \fIcount\fR times.
.TP
.B IRP \fIparam\fR,<\fIvalue\fR[,\fIvalue\fR]...>
Repeat block for each value.
.TP
.B IRPC \fIparam\fR,\fIstring\fR
Repeat block for each character in string.
.SS Conditional Assembly
.TP
.B IF \fIexpression\fR
Assemble following code if expression is non-zero.
.TP
.B ELSE
Assemble following code if IF was false.
.TP
.B ENDIF
End conditional block.
.TP
.B IFDEF \fIsymbol\fR
Assemble if symbol is defined.
.TP
.B IFNDEF \fIsymbol\fR
Assemble if symbol is not defined.
.SS Listing Control
.TP
.B TITLE \fItext\fR
Set listing title.
.TP
.B PAGE
Start new listing page.
.TP
.B .LIST
Enable listing output.
.TP
.B .XLIST
Disable listing output.
.SS Processor Mode
.TP
.B .Z80
Enable Z80 instruction set.
.TP
.B .8080
Restrict to 8080 instruction set (default).
.SS Include Files
.TP
.B INCLUDE \fIfilename\fR
Include another source file.
.SH EXPRESSIONS
Expressions can include:
.IP \(bu 2
Decimal numbers (default)
.IP \(bu 2
Hexadecimal numbers (suffix H or prefix 0x)
.IP \(bu 2
Octal numbers (suffix O or Q)
.IP \(bu 2
Binary numbers (suffix B)
.IP \(bu 2
ASCII characters in quotes ('A')
.IP \(bu 2
Symbols
.IP \(bu 2
Current location counter ($)
.PP
Operators: + \- * / MOD AND OR XOR NOT SHL SHR HIGH LOW
.PP
An expression whose value depends on where the linker places a segment or on
an external symbol, and that is not simply an address plus a constant (for
example HIGH or LOW of a relocatable label, or any relocatable or external
value in a one-byte field), is passed to the linker as REL extension link
items, as MACRO-80 3.44 does. AND, OR, XOR, SHL, SHR and the comparisons have
no linker operator and are errors on such a value in an instruction, DB or DW.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Assemble with default output:
.B um80 program.mac
.TP
Specify output file:
.B um80 \-o code.rel program.mac
.TP
Generate listing:
.B um80 \-l program.prn program.mac
.TP
Define symbol:
.B um80 \-D DEBUG=1 program.mac
.TP
Add include path:
.B um80 \-I /usr/share/include program.mac
.TP
Export all symbols for debugging:
.B um80 \-g program.mac
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful assembly (may include warnings).
.TP
.B 1
Assembly failed due to errors.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I input.mac
Assembly source file.
.TP
.I output.rel
Relocatable object file.
.TP
.I output.prn
Assembly listing (if requested).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ul80 (1),
.BR ulib80 (1),
.BR ucref80 (1),
.BR ud80 (1)
.PP
The original Microsoft MACRO-80 manual is available at docs/external/m80.pdf
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.B um80
is compatible with Microsoft MACRO-80 (M80) with the following notes:
.IP \(bu 2
Source files can use Unix (LF) or DOS (CR/LF) line endings.
.IP \(bu 2
Symbol names are converted to uppercase.
.IP \(bu 2
The .REL file format is binary-compatible with the original: LINK-80 3.44
loads um80's objects. LINK-80 refuses a symbol of more than 7 characters
(more than 6 for an external inside a link-time expression), which um80
writes in an extended form only ul80 reads; M80 itself keeps 6, as
.B \-t
does.
.SH AUTHOR
um80 project contributors.
